# Desktop Runtime Compatibility trial run notes

Start by focusing the desktop runtime checks on `js/backend/Electron.js`. This pass did not cover the export pipeline; the goal is to identify which local capabilities the backend entry point depends on at runtime, then decide where to put the test inputs.

Currently visible at the top of the file:

```js
import {AbstractBackend, addBackend} from "./AbstractBackend";
import fs from "fs";
import path from "path";
import process from "process";
```

`AbstractBackend` and `addBackend` are the two nearby references to revisit first. `fs`, `path`, and `process` divide the checks into three areas: file access, path resolution, and the process environment; don't change all three in a single experiment.

First-run notes still to add: use the same presentation, change only the desktop runtime, check whether the backend finishes registering, and record the startup result. If there is no result here, return to the call site for `addBackend`; don't infer the actual execution order from the import list.

# Desktop Runtime Compatibility trial run notes

The review still starts at `js/backend/Electron.js`. This is a good place to keep desktop runtime check notes: it touches the backend abstraction, local file paths, and the process object, but this review did not expand to a full check of export functionality.

The dependencies at the top of the file are below, for cross-checking the next time you open the code:

```js
import {AbstractBackend, addBackend} from "./AbstractBackend";
import fs from "fs";
import path from "path";
import process from "process";
```

The review confirmed two small points. First, `Electron.js` is not a standalone runtime script; follow `addBackend` to continue tracing backend registration. Second, the filesystem, paths, and process environment appear at the same entry point, so the experiment notes must state which one was held fixed at the time. The earlier draft treated "these imports are visible" as an established compatibility finding; that assessment is not being retained.

No desktop startup logs were saved in this round, and no passing result was recorded for a specific runtime version. The results field therefore goes no further than this: the entry point and observation points have been identified; compatibility has not yet been determined.

In the next experiment, change only the desktop runtime, using the same presentation and the same set of local paths. Check in this order: whether the backend registers through `addBackend`; whether reads involving `fs` return; whether the path still points to the original file after being processed by `path`; whether the environment information provided by `process` changes that branch. If any of the four shows a difference, post the output for that item first; don't adjust the other three together.

Resource cleanup and ffmpeg selection are documented separately in DOC-26; the only scope boundary retained here is that they should not be mixed into this runtime experiment.
